---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---

<Base title="About" description={site.description}>
  <div class="wrap" style="padding-block: 2.5rem; max-width: 47rem;">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <div class="post-body">
      <p>{site.footerAbout}</p>

      <h2>Who it is for</h2>
      <p>
        People who will actually clone the thing: engineers, platform teams, anyone deciding what
        to bring into a stack. The assumption is that you can read a README yourself, so the post
        spends its words on what the README leaves out.
      </p>

      <h2>What gets published</h2>
      <p>
        Two projects a day, one post each, around five hundred words. A repository qualifies when
        something changed recently enough to explain the attention: a first stable release, a
        rewrite, a spike in stars, a licence change. Star count alone is not a story.
      </p>

      <h2>What does not get published</h2>
      <p>
        Awesome-lists, roadmaps, interview-prep repositories, course notes and cheat sheets. They
        collect stars faster than software does, and covering them is how a site about GitHub
        stops being about code.
      </p>

      <h2>How it is written</h2>
      <p>The details are on the <a href="/disclosure">disclosure page</a>.</p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Corrections, tips and takedown requests: open an issue on the site repository, or write to
        the address listed there.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
